---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatCssUnits from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatCssUnits.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="px-to-percent">
  <DevFormatCssUnits client:load from="px" to="percent" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Percentages are relative to different things</h2>
    <p>
      This is the single most important thing to know about percentages in CSS, and it is why they cause
      more layout surprises than any other unit. The reference depends on the property.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Percentage is of…</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>font-size</code></td><td>The parent&rsquo;s font size. The converter above computes this</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>width</code>, <code>max-width</code>, <code>left</code>, <code>right</code></td><td>The containing block&rsquo;s width</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>height</code>, <code>top</code>, <code>bottom</code></td><td>The containing block&rsquo;s height. Ignored if that height is <code>auto</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>padding</code> and <code>margin</code>, all four sides</td><td>The containing block&rsquo;s width, also for top and bottom</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>line-height</code></td><td>The element&rsquo;s own font size</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>transform: translate()</code></td><td>The element&rsquo;s own size</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>background-position</code></td><td>The positioning area minus the image size</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>border-width</code></td><td>Not allowed. Percentages are invalid here</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The padding row is the famous one, and it is genuinely useful: <code>padding-top: 56.25%</code> on
      an empty box produces a 16:9 aspect ratio, because 9/16 = 0.5625 of the width. Modern CSS has
      <code>aspect-ratio</code> for this, but the trick is still everywhere in older code.
    </p>

    <h2>Percentage font sizing</h2>
    <pre><code>% = px ÷ parent font size × 100

Parent 16px:
  14px → 87.5%
  18px → 112.5%
  20px → 125%</code></pre>
    <p>
      A percentage font size is arithmetically identical to em: <code>87.5%</code> and
      <code>0.875em</code> compute to exactly the same value. So it compounds through nesting in exactly
      the same way. Four nested levels of 90% leave you at 65.6% of where you started.
    </p>

    <h2>Where percentage font sizing is still standard</h2>
    <p>
      On the root element, where it means something rem and em cannot:
    </p>
    <pre><code>html &#123; font-size: 100%; &#125;   /* respect the user's chosen size */
html &#123; font-size: 62.5%; &#125;  /* the "1rem = 10px" trick */</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>100%</code> is the honest default. It is also the reason not to write
      <code>html &#123; font-size: 16px &#125;</code>: that pins the root to 16 regardless of what the user
      asked for, which defeats the whole point of using rem below it.
    </p>

    <h2>Converting a width to a percentage</h2>
    <p>
      Set the parent field to the container&rsquo;s width in pixels rather than a font size, and read the
      percentage. A 320px sidebar inside a 1200px container is 26.6667%.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two caveats before you use that number. Percentage widths do not account for gaps, padding or
      borders unless <code>box-sizing: border-box</code> is set, so three columns at 33.3333% with a gap
      will wrap. In most modern layouts you do not need to compute this: CSS Grid
      (<code>grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr</code>) and Flexbox handle the arithmetic and the gaps for
      you.
    </p>

    <h2>Repeating decimals</h2>
    <p>
      A third is 33.333…%, and where you truncate matters. Browsers work in sub-pixel precision, so
      33.3333% is close enough that rounding is invisible; 33.33% across three columns of a 1200px
      container leaves a 0.36px gap, which sounds like nothing and shows up as a one-pixel seam against a
      contrasting background. Use four decimal places, or use <code>fr</code> units and let the layout
      engine distribute the remainder exactly.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
